<Base
  title="Research Program · Xiaochuan Li"
  description="A research program on how structured meaning is represented, processed, and learned, connecting formal semantics with psycholinguistic and computational evidence."
>
  <article class="rp">
    <h1 class="rp-h1">Research Program</h1>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">Linguistic meaning and inference</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        My work asks how linguistic form shapes the alternatives available for interpretation and
        what happens to them in later reasoning. A description can make some possible referents more
        likely than others; an epistemic expression can present a proposition as possible while
        leaving its truth unsettled. These are related questions about interpretation, but they do
        not assume one shared mechanism.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">Three roles, three kinds of evidence</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        <strong>Linguistic analysis</strong> specifies what expressions mean, which alternatives they
        make available, and what inferences they license. It turns an intuitive contrast into
        explicit predictions. It can show what a theory says, but by itself it cannot show how
        people or models behave.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        <strong>Human experiments</strong> test whether a linguistic contrast changes how people
        interpret, remember, or reason about those alternatives. They provide evidence about human
        cognition. A proposed study can follow from an analysis, but it remains a proposal until it
        is run and its results are reported.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        <strong>Language-model experiments</strong> test what a model can learn from specified input,
        whether it generalises beyond surface cues, and which information guides its choices. When
        behaviour is controlled carefully, internal interventions can further test which model
        representations contribute to an answer. This evidence is about the tested models and setup.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">How the evidence connects</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        The three routes can constrain one another: an analysis guides what to test; human and model
        results can support, challenge, or refine its predictions; and a mismatch can expose a
        missing distinction or a limitation in the experiment. But they answer different questions.
        A model result does not establish how people understand language, and a human result does not
        establish what a model learned.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">Where the current projects fit</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        The typicality projects show how the roles differ. <a href="/research/typicality-in-referent-choice">Typicality
        in referent choice</a> is a linguistic analysis of what a forced choice between candidate
        referents can establish; it reports no new experiment. <a href="/research/cross-lingual-protobias">Cross-lingual
        ProtoBias</a> is a completed language-model study testing whether models follow explicit
        semantic constraints when they conflict with typicality, and whether choices vary across
        prompt languages. Its results are evidence about those models and materials, not about human
        interpretation.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        <a href="/research/three-ways">Three Ways of Leaving <em>p</em> Unsettled</a> compares what
        epistemic expressions contribute to discourse. A related <a href="/research/learnability">human-focused
        thesis</a> is under discussion; it could test whether the analysed differences affect
        people's later access to and use of a possibility. That experiment has not begun. <a href="/research/latent-control-states">Latent
        Control States</a> develops methods for testing which internal model computations contribute
        to a choice; those mechanistic tests are also still in development.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">Scope and progress</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        The shared programme is a way of asking and testing questions, not a claim that every project
        contains all three kinds of evidence. Each study has its own question and completion status;
        comparisons are useful when their limits are kept explicit.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="rp-foot"><a href="/research">See how this program is being developed in the current work →</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

